  "writeup_markdown": "---\ntitle: \"Embu\"\nsubtitle: \"Kenya\"\nregion: \"Sub Saharan Africa\"\ntags: [ethnography, sub-saharan-africa]\n---\n\n## At a glance\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Who | The Embu, a Bantu people who call themselves Aembu |\n| Where | Embu County on the southern slopes of Mount Kenya in Kenya |\n| How many | Population not recorded in sources |\n| Language | Kĩembu, a Bantu language related to Kikuyu, Kimeru and Kamba |\n| Religion | Traditionally belief in Ngai (God); most are now Christian, some Muslim |\n| Known for | Ritual and healing specialists' equipment · Beaded leather headgear and ornaments · Agile dance with acrobatic body movements · Fertile Mount Kenya highland agriculture · Origin myths centered at Mwenendega grove |\n\n## Overview\n\nThe Embu are a Bantu people living on Mount Kenya's fertile southern slopes in Kenya's Eastern Province, bordered by the Kikuyu, Meru and Kamba. They consist of two subgroups: the Embu proper and the semi-arid Mbeere. Most are now Christian, though they traditionally worshipped Ngai (God) and the spirits of ancestors. They built their livelihood on highland agriculture growing maize, beans, yams and bananas.\n\n## Material culture\n\nEmbu material culture is dominated by wooden, hide, horn, gourd and bamboo objects, with weapons and ritual specialists' equipment comprising the main museum collections.\n\n### Textile & pattern traditions\n\nNo weaving tradition is recorded; pattern is found mainly in beaded and shell-covered leather ornaments.\n\n- **Cowrie shell and bead ornaments** — Leather headdresses and head-rings faced with cowrie shells, glass beads and shell beads.\n- **Bead-covered pendants** — A head-band of leather hung with pendants covered in red and pink beads.\n- **Reed and seed fringe** — A headdress fringe made of reeds and seeds strung on a plaited fiber cord.\n- **Pigmented shield** — An oval shield of bark covered with red pigment.\n\n### Clothing & dress\n\nEmbu dress is documented only through elaborate head ornaments combining marine shells, glass beads and leather.\n\n- **Head-rings** — Leather head-rings faced with cowrie shells, glass beads and shell beads.\n- **Beaded head-band** — A leather head-band hung with pendants covered in red and pink beads.\n- **Head-dress fringe** — Reeds and seeds threaded onto a plaited vegetable-fiber cord.\n- **Ear-plug** — A wooden ear-plug indicating pierced and distended earlobes.\n\n### Architecture\n\nEmbu architecture is not described in available sources.\n\n### Ceramics, metalwork & everyday objects\n\nEmbu objects are chiefly made from wood, hide, horn, gourd and bamboo, with weapons and ritual equipment dominating collections.\n\n- **Sword** — A sword made of iron, wood and hide.\n- **Bow** — A bow of wood strung with vegetable fiber, sometimes bound with haired skin.\n- **Game arrows** — Arrows of wood, reed, feather and vegetable fiber.\n- **Shield** — An oval shield of bark under red pigment and a wooden parrying shield.\n- **Club or knobkerrie** — A striking weapon with a stone head sheathed in hide on a wooden handle.\n\n### Jewelry & body adornment\n\nEmbu body adornment centers on beaded leather headgear and shell work, with no recorded metal jewelry, tattooing or scarification.\n\n- **Beaded leather headgear** — Cowrie shells, glass beads and shell beads worked onto leather rings and bands.\n- **Beaded pendants** — Pendants covered with red and pink beads.\n- **Wooden ear-plug** — A wooden plug indicating pierced and distended earlobes.\n\n## Music & performance\n\nEmbu musical instruments recorded are wooden war horns; the Embu are known to love music and dance, though specific genres and ensembles are not documented.\n\n- **War horn** — A wooden side-blown horn used as a signaling instrument for territorial defense.\n- **Carved war horn** — A second carved wooden side-blown war horn.\n\n## Dance & theatre\n\nEmbu dance is known for agility, acrobatics and body movements resembling those of the Akamba, but no individual dances or dramatic traditions are named.\n\n## Festivals & rituals\n\nNo annual festivals or life-cycle rituals are documented; the record instead details extensive equipment of ritual and healing specialists.\n\n- **Wizard's bag** — A bag cut from a single piece of folded hide and stitched with a carrying strap, used by ritual specialists.\n- **Medicinal powder tubes** — Bamboo tubes holding medicinal powder and chalk powder for ceremonies.\n- **Medicine vessels** — Gourd vessels of medicine and medicinal powder, variously mounted with skin and decorated with hide bands.\n- **Divination powder vessel** — A gourd and skin vessel containing divination powder.\n- **Horn charms** — Charms made of horn, some from gerenuk antelope, others combining horn with hide, wood, skin, claws and conch shell.\n\n## Foodways\n\nEmbu agriculture on Mount Kenya's windward slopes yields two growing seasons yearly, supporting diverse crops and livestock.\n\n- **Nyeni** — Traditional vegetables.\n- **Mukimo** — Mashed banana with traditional vegetables or potatoes.\n- **Githere** (*githere or muthere*) — Unfettered corn seeds cooked with beans or peas and traditional vegetables.\n- **Nyama cia gwakia** — Roasted meat.\n- **Irio** — Mashed dry beans, corn and potatoes.\n\n## Oral tradition & literature\n\nThe Embu preserve an origin myth centered at the Mwenendega grove, telling of a divine wife and the founding of their land.\n\n- **Mwenendega origin myth** (*Mwenendega*) — A creation story set at the Mwenendega grove near Runyenjes, in which Ngai created Mwenendega and gave him a divine wife at the Gogo River Salt Lick.\n- **Ciũrũnjĩ** (*Ciũrũnjĩ or Nthara*) — The divine wife sent by God, who made Mwenendega swear never to abuse her.\n- **Mount Kenya naming** (*Mwenenyaga or Mwenenjerũ*) — Mount Kenya is regarded as the seat of the lord and owner of the snow, from words meaning 'white'.\n\n## Language & religion\n\nThe Embu speak Kĩembu, a Bantu language with two dialects; traditionally they worshipped Ngai and ancestors, now mostly Christian.\n\n- **Kĩembu language** (*Kĩembu*) — A Bantu language of the Niger-Congo family with two main dialects, Embu proper and Mbeere, and a Mount Kenya variety called Kirũgũrũ.\n- **Ngai** (*Ngai or Murungu*) — A single invisible and transcendent God living in the sky, omnipotent creator of life, merciful but distant.\n- **Ancestor veneration** — The spirits of the departed are regarded as intercessors between the living and Ngai, remembered in family offerings and libations at individual altars.\n\n## Glossary\n\n- *Aembu* — The Embu people (singular Muembu)\n- *Kĩembu* — The Bantu language spoken by the Embu\n- *Mwenendega* — A founder figure in Embu origin myth\n- *Ciũrũnjĩ* — The divine wife in the origin myth, also called Nthara\n- *Ngai* — God in Embu tradition, also called Murungu\n- *Nyeni* — Traditional vegetables\n- *Mukimo* — Mashed banana with vegetables or potatoes\n- *Githere* — Corn seeds cooked with beans and vegetables, also muthere\n- *Nyama cia gwakia* — Roasted meat\n- *Irio* — Mashed dry beans, corn and potatoes\n- *Kirũgũrũ* — A variety of Kĩembu spoken near Mount Kenya\n- *Mwenenyaga* — The lord and owner of the snow, a name for Mount Kenya\n- *Mwenenjerũ* — Another name for the owner of Mount Kenya's snow\n- *Gogo River Salt Lick* — The location of Mukuuri, where the divine wife was found\n- *Mbeere* — A subgroup of the Embu occupying semi-arid areas\n\n## Sources & further reading\n\n- Henry Stanley Kabeca Mwaniki, Embu Historical Texts. Kampala: East African Literature Bureau, 1974.\n- Ciarunji Chesaina, Oral Literature of the Embu and Mbeere. East African Publishers, 1997.\n- H. S. K. Mwaniki, Mbeere Historical Texts, 2005 — the source of the Mwenendega and Ciũrũnjĩ narrative cited above.\n- Angelique Haugerud, The Culture of Politics in Modern Kenya. Cambridge University Press, 1997.\n- Godfrey Mwakikagile, Life in Kenya: The Land and the People, Modern and Traditional Ways. New Africa Press, 2010.\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embu_people\n- https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Embu_language\n- British Museum online collection — the Embu weaponry, beaded headgear and ritual practitioners' equipment described above: https://www.britishmuseum.org/collection\n\n",
